<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Älvsborg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Älvsborg Fortress</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Älvsborg was a Swedish fortress at the mouth of the Göta älv river, near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothenburg">Gothenburg</a>, guarding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>'s only outlet to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> captured it twice and returned it for large ransoms, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-stettin-1570">Treaty of Stettin</a> in 1570 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-knared">Treaty of Knäred</a> in 1613. The fortress of Nya Älvsborg was later built on an island at the river mouth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alvsborg.jpg" alt="Älvsborg" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Saftgurka · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C3%84lvsborgs_Fortress_(Oscar_II%27s_fort)_in_Gothenburg.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
